<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Tracon.Ui.E2ETests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Playwright" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Tracon.UI/Tracon.UI.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Tracon.Workflows/Tracon.Workflows.csproj" />
    <!-- Fake model provider (FakeModelProvider): Phase 39, K-007 rationale section 39.1. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Tracon.Testing/Tracon.Testing.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    🚨 The run profile, not a single flaky test. Six timing failures across four
    projects were measured in full-solution runs and every one of them passed
    in isolation: a live voice loop that missed a 60-second window, a Playwright
    click that timed out, a Native AOT publish whose compiler crashed, and
    concurrent database writers. `-maxcpucount:1` serializes the test PROJECTS
    (measured: one test process at a time) but says nothing about the threads
    INSIDE one, and xunit defaults to one per processor. This caps them, so a
    project cannot saturate the machine on its own.

    Phase 184: until then the cap was moot here - all console tests lived in
    ONE class, and xunit runs the tests of one class one at a time. Split into
    one class per screen, the classes now run four at a time. Measured before
    adopting it: 5 of 5 project runs green in 35-48 s against 93-99 s serial,
    after fixing the two test races the parallel runs exposed (a voice commit
    sent before any audio, a badge count read before its list loaded).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="xunit.runner.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
